---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = "Keys";
const description = "My public keys: age for encryption, SSH for signatures, Ariadne for identity, PGP for compatibility.";

// Only public key material ever appears on this page. Secrets live in 1Password.
const AGE_CLASSIC = "age187ce86uaqypfhmz55rfy630s9dp9v6s0v028mgf554m0094tmdcqw3fufl";
const ASPE_URI = "aspe:keyoxide.org:TDJ7KADY5LUZNFIFGWYETVL5TU";
const KEYOXIDE_PROFILE = `https://keyoxide.org/${ASPE_URI}`;
const GIST_PROOF = "https://gist.github.com/agucova/13feec4b34b49d1a78abe49f8d01c59a";
const PGP_FINGERPRINT = "503B 2899 F90F 9FE5 BF33 1E3C D0B5 DA36 0A0D A1E0";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <p>
        Everything here is public key material, served from my own domain.
        Nothing on this page is secret, and every secret half lives offline.
        If you want to send me something private, use <a href="#encrypt">age</a>.
        If you want to check that something came from me, use
        <a href="#signatures">my SSH signing keys</a>. If you want to check that
        this page is really mine, see <a href="#identity">identity</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="encrypt">Encrypt something to me</h2>

    <p>
        <a href="https://age-encryption.org" rel="noopener noreferrer">age</a> is
        the easiest way to send me something private. My default key is a hybrid
        post-quantum one, so you will need age 1.3 or newer. It mixes ordinary
        and post-quantum cryptography, which keeps your message private even if
        someone saves it today and tries to crack it decades from now.
    </p>

    <pre><code># encrypt a file to me
curl -s https://agucova.dev/age.txt | age -R - -o secret.age file.pdf</code></pre>

    <details>
        <summary>The recipient itself (about 2 KB, ML-KEM public keys are large)</summary>
        <textarea class="key-wrapper" rows="10" readonly="true" onclick="this.focus();this.select()"></textarea>
    </details>

    <h3>Classic X25519, for older tooling</h3>

    <p>
        If your age is older than 1.3, or your tool does not know about the
        post-quantum key yet, use this one instead. It is short enough to paste:
    </p>

    <pre><code>{AGE_CLASSIC}</code></pre>

    <pre><code># same thing, classic recipient
curl -s https://agucova.dev/age-classic.txt | age -R - -o secret.age file.pdf</code></pre>

    <h3>With my SSH keys instead</h3>

    <p>
        age also accepts SSH public keys as recipients, so you can encrypt to me
        with nothing but my GitHub username.
    </p>

    <pre><code>curl -s https://github.com/agucova.keys &gt; agus.keys
age -R agus.keys -o secret.age file.pdf</code></pre>

    <h3>For a conversation</h3>

    <p>
        Static keys are for files and one-off messages. For an actual
        conversation, use <a href="https://signal.org/">Signal</a>:
        <code>@agucova.42</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="identity">Identity</h2>

    <p>
        My accounts are tied together with an
        <a href="https://docs.keyoxide.org/wiki/ariadne-identity/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ariadne</a>
        profile, which you can verify with
        <a href={KEYOXIDE_PROFILE} rel="noopener noreferrer">Keyoxide</a> or any
        other implementation. Verification is a public algorithm: each claimed
        account publishes a link back to the profile, and you can check those
        links yourself without trusting me or Keyoxide.
    </p>

    <pre><code>{ASPE_URI}</code></pre>

    <p>The backlinks, if you want to check them by hand:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>This domain: <code>dig +short TXT agucova.dev</code></li>
        <li>GitHub: <a href={GIST_PROOF} rel="noopener noreferrer">gist</a></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="signatures">Verify something I signed</h2>

    <p>
        I sign with SSH keys. One key per device, all of them listed in
        <a href="/allowed_signers">allowed_signers</a>, and also available at
        <a href="https://github.com/agucova.keys" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/agucova.keys</a>.
    </p>

    <pre><code>curl -sO https://agucova.dev/allowed_signers
ssh-keygen -Y verify -f allowed_signers -I agustin@agucova.dev \
    -n file -s document.pdf.sig &lt; document.pdf</code></pre>

    <h2>PGP</h2>

    <p>
        My PGP key still works and I still read encrypted mail, but I would
        rather you used age. I keep it for people and tools that only speak
        OpenPGP. It is the same key I have used since 2021, and I do not plan to
        replace it.
    </p>

    <p>Fingerprint: <code>{PGP_FINGERPRINT}</code></p>

    <pre><code>curl https://agucova.dev/pgp.asc | gpg --import</code></pre>

    <details>
        <summary>The key itself</summary>
        <textarea class="pgp-wrapper" rows="15" readonly="true" onclick="this.focus();this.select()"></textarea>
    </details>

    <script>
        // Bulky key material is fetched instead of inlined, so the page stays
        // small for the majority of visitors who never open these.
        const loadInto = (selector: string, path: string) => {
            const target = document.querySelector(selector);
            if (!target) return;
            fetch(path)
                .then((response) => response.text())
                .then((text) => {
                    target.textContent = text.trim();
                })
                .catch(() => {
                    target.textContent = `Could not load ${path}. Try opening it directly.`;
                });
        };

        loadInto(".key-wrapper", "/age.txt");
        loadInto(".pgp-wrapper", "/pgp.asc");
    </script>

    <style>
        textarea.key-wrapper,
        textarea.pgp-wrapper {
            width: 100%;
            box-sizing: border-box;
            font-family: monospace;
            font-size: 0.8rem;
            white-space: pre;
        }

        pre code {
            white-space: pre-wrap;
            word-break: break-all;
        }
    </style>
</Layout>
